data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The French","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Revolution (1789–1799) stands as one of the most transformative","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" events in modern history. It dismantled the centuries-old feudal order, challenged the divine right of kings, and established the foundational","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" principles of modern democracy. Its causes were a volatile mixture of economic desperation, social inequality, and intellectual awakening, while its consequences resh","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"aped the political map of Europe and the world.\n\n### The Causes: A System in Collapse\n\nThe roots of the French","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Revolution were deeply embedded in the structural failures of the *Ancien Régime*. By the late 18th century, France","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" was governed by a rigid social hierarchy divided into three \"Estates.\" The First Estate (clergy) and the Second Estate (nobility","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":") owned the vast majority of the land, held the most prestigious government positions, and were largely exempt from taxation. The","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Third Estate, comprising 98% of the population—ranging from wealthy merchants and lawyers to impoverished peasants—bore the entire tax","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" burden while possessing no political power.\n\nEconomic crisis acted as the primary catalyst. France had exhausted its treasury through decades of warfare","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", most notably by financing the American Revolution. Simultaneously, a series of poor harvests in the late 1780s","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" led to widespread famine and skyrocketing bread prices. As the peasantry starved, the monarchy under King Louis XVI appeared indifferent, living","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" in opulence at Versailles while the state teetered on the brink of bankruptcy.\n\nIntellectual currents further destabilized the monarchy","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". The Enlightenment, led by thinkers like Voltaire, Rousseau, and Montesquieu, challenged the traditional authority of the Church","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" and the Crown. These philosophers championed concepts of popular sovereignty, natural rights, and the social contract. The educated bourgeoisie,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" frustrated by their exclusion from power, adopted these ideas to argue that the government existed to serve the people, not the other","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" way around.\n\nWhen Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General in 1789 to address the financial crisis, the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Third Estate broke away, declaring itself the National Assembly. This act of defiance, followed by the storming of the Bastille on July 1","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"4, 1789, signaled the end of absolute monarchy and the beginning of a revolutionary struggle for \"Liberty, Equality, and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Fraternity.\"\n\n### The Consequences: A New World Order\n\nThe consequences of the French Revolution were profound, immediate, and enduring","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". \n\n**1. The End of Feudalism and Absolute Monarchy**\nThe Revolution effectively abolished the feudal","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" system. The *Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen* (1789) codified the end","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of aristocratic privilege and established that all men were born free and equal in rights. The monarchy was eventually abolished, and Louis","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" XVI was executed in 1793, signaling that the king was no longer a divinely ordained ruler but a public","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" servant subject to the law.\n\n**2. The Rise of Nationalism**\nThe Revolution transformed the concept of the \"subject","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\" into the \"citizen.\" For the first time, people identified not with a monarch, but with the nation itself. This surge","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of nationalism was bolstered by the *levée en masse* (mass conscription), which created a citizen-army.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" This sense of national identity would become a defining feature of modern statehood, fueling independence movements across Europe and, eventually, the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" globe.\n\n**3. The Spread of Liberalism and Secularism**\nThe Revolution promoted secularism by stripping the Catholic","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Church of its vast landholdings and political influence. It introduced the Napoleonic Code, which standardized laws, protected property rights","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", and promoted meritocracy. Although Napoleon Bonaparte eventually seized power and declared himself Emperor, he exported these revolutionary ideals across Europe through","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" his military conquests, permanently weakening the foundations of absolute monarchies elsewhere.\n\n**4. The Precedent of Radical Change","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"**\nThe Revolution also demonstrated the dangers of political extremism. The \"Reign of Terror,\" led by Maximilien Robespierre,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" showed how the pursuit of ideological purity could lead to mass violence and authoritarianism. This duality—the promise of democratic liberation versus the reality","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of state-sponsored violence—became a central theme in political discourse for the next two centuries.\n\n**5. Global Impact**","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\nThe ripple effects of the French Revolution were felt far beyond Europe. It inspired the Haitian Revolution, the first successful slave revolt in","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the Americas, and provided the intellectual framework for the Latin American wars of independence led by figures like Simón Bolívar. It forced","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" existing monarchies to reform or face their own destruction, setting the stage for the rise of representative governments in the 19th and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" 20th centuries.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe French Revolution was not merely a change of government; it was a fundamental","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" shift in the human experience. While it was characterized by chaos, bloodshed, and the eventual rise of a military dictatorship, its","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" legacy remains the bedrock of modern political life. By asserting that sovereignty resides in the people and that all citizens possess inherent rights, the Revolution provided","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the blueprint for the democratic world. It taught humanity that while the path to progress is often violent and unpredictable, the desire","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" for equality and self-determination is an unstoppable force in history.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

